<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Resources that are only ever looked up by *name* at runtime, and which the resource shrinker
  therefore believes nobody uses.

  `isShrinkResources = true` decides what to keep by looking for references from code and from
  other resources. A `resources.getIdentifier("some_name", …)` call is an ordinary string as far as
  that analysis is concerned, so anything reached that way is invisible to it and gets deleted from
  the release build only. Nothing warns; the app simply behaves differently once shrunk.

  Both entries below were verified missing from a release APK:

  * `default_web_client_id` — generated by the google-services plugin and read by
    `GoogleSignIn.android.kt`. Its removal is what crashed internal testing with
    "default_web_client_id is missing… check that the plugin is applied", which is a misleading
    message: the plugin was applied and had generated the string correctly. The shrinker removed
    it afterwards.

  * `ic_notification` — read by `PassAlertReceiver`, which lives in `core:notifications` and so
    cannot reference the app module's `R`. It falls back to the launcher icon rather than
    crashing, so this one was silent: pass reminders showed a full-colour launcher icon where
    Android expects a white-on-transparent notification icon, which renders as a grey blob.

  The remaining `google_*` strings are listed defensively. They survived this build, but Firebase
  reads them through the same name-based mechanism, so their survival is a property of the current
  dependency graph rather than something guaranteed.
-->
<resources
    xmlns:tools="http://schemas.android.com/tools"
    tools:keep="
        @string/default_web_client_id,
        @string/google_app_id,
        @string/google_api_key,
        @string/google_crash_reporting_api_key,
        @string/google_storage_bucket,
        @string/gcm_defaultSenderId,
        @string/project_id,
        @drawable/ic_notification" />
